# The `permissions` API # {#permissions-api}

The <dfn id="permissions-api-concept">permissions</dfn> API lets an extension check, request, and relinquish its own
[=optional permissions=] and [=optional host permissions=] at runtime, instead of requiring
every permission at install time.

An <dfn>optional permission</dfn> is a permission the extension may hold
without it being granted at install: one listed in `optional_permissions`,
or one listed in `permissions` that the user agent has withheld until the
extension requests it.

A named permission required by the extension's manifest, or optional and
granted to the extension at runtime, is part of the extension's <dfn>granted
permissions</dfn>.

## Types ## {#permissions-types}

### The `Permissions` dictionary ### {#permissions-dictionary}

A {{Permissions}} dictionary's `permissions` member is a list of named API permission strings,
excluding host permissions. Its `origins` member is a list of [=match pattern|match patterns=],
validated using the same [=match pattern=] grammar used for the `host_permissions` manifest key.

Which strings are valid entries in `permissions` is implementation-defined: each browser
recognizes its own set of permission names, and a name outside a given browser's set is not a
valid entry for that browser. An entry in `permissions` or `origins` that the user agent does
not recognize is an error. No permission is granted or revoked, and the error is surfaced to
the caller.

<div class=note>
These sixteen permission names are recognized by Chrome, Firefox and Safari alike:

* `activeTab`
* `alarms`
* `clipboardWrite`
* `contextMenus`
* `cookies`
* `declarativeNetRequest`
* `declarativeNetRequestFeedback`
* `declarativeNetRequestWithHostAccess`
* `nativeMessaging`
* `notifications`
* `scripting`
* `storage`
* `tabs`
* `unlimitedStorage`
* `webNavigation`
* `webRequest`

A name outside this list is recognized by at most two of the three, so it is not portable.
</div>

<div class=issue>
Should an unrecognized name be signalled by throwing synchronously or by
rejecting the returned promise?

* Chrome: rejects the returned promise.
* Firefox: throws synchronously.
* Safari: throws synchronously for `contains()`, but rejects the promise
    for `request()` and `remove()`.

A synchronous throw escapes a `.catch()` attached to the returned promise, so
`browser.permissions.contains(bad).then(ok).catch(err)` behaves differently across browsers.
</div>

<pre class="idl">
dictionary Permissions {
  sequence&lt;DOMString&gt; permissions;
  sequence&lt;DOMString&gt; origins;
};
</pre>

## Methods ## {#permissions-methods}

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=WebExtensions]
interface permissions {
  Promise&lt;Permissions&gt; getAll();
  Promise&lt;boolean&gt; contains(optional Permissions permissions);
  Promise&lt;boolean&gt; request(optional Permissions permissions);
  Promise&lt;undefined&gt; remove(optional Permissions permissions);

  readonly attribute PermissionsEvent onAdded;
  readonly attribute PermissionsEvent onRemoved;
};
</pre>

### `getAll()` ### {#permissions-getall}

<dfn method for="permissions">getAll()</dfn> returns the extension's complete current set of
permissions and [=match pattern|match patterns=]: its [=granted permissions=] and [=granted host
permissions=].

1. Let |result| be a new {{Permissions}}.
1. Set |result|["permissions"] to the extension's [=granted permissions=].
1. Set |result|["origins"] to the extension's [=granted host permissions=].
1. Resolve with |result|.

Issue: May `getAll()` return an all-hosts pattern that no single granted
permission contains, when the granted patterns together cover all hosts?
Safari does, returning `*://*/*`. Chrome and Firefox return only what was
granted.

### `contains()` ### {#permissions-contains}

<dfn method for="permissions">contains(permissions)</dfn> checks whether the extension's
[=granted permissions=] and [=granted host permissions=] include every permission and origin
named in |permissions|.

1. For each named permission in |permissions|["permissions"]:
    1. If it is not in the extension's [=granted permissions=], resolve with `false`.
1. For each [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"]:
    1. If it is not [=subsumed=] by the extension's [=granted host permissions=], resolve with
        `false`.
1. Resolve with `true`.

### `request()` ### {#permissions-request}

<dfn method for="permissions">request(permissions)</dfn> asks the user to grant the extension
some or all of |permissions| that are not already part of its [=granted permissions=] or
[=granted host permissions=].

1. If this call is not happening within a [=user activation=], reject with an error.
1. If any named permission in |permissions|["permissions"] is not an [=optional permission=] of
    the extension, reject with an error.
1. If any [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"] is not [=subsumed=] by an [=optional host
    permission=] of the extension, reject with an error.
1. If every named permission in |permissions|["permissions"] is in the extension's [=granted
    permissions=], and every [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"] is [=subsumed=] by the
    extension's [=granted host permissions=], resolve with `true`.
1. Prompt the user to approve the permissions and origins in |permissions| that are not already
    part of the extension's [=granted permissions=] or [=granted host permissions=]. User agents
    may skip the prompt, and grant automatically, when the newly requested permissions and
    origins carry no additional user-facing warnings beyond what the extension is already
    granted.
1. If the user does not approve the prompt, resolve with `false`.
1. Grant the extension the requested permissions and origins not already in its [=granted
    permissions=] or [=granted host permissions=]. This fires {{permissions/onAdded}} for the
    newly granted set.
1. Resolve with `true`.

Issue: What does `request()` reject with, for a missing user activation, an
unlisted permission, or an unlisted origin? Chrome rejects with an `Error`
carrying a fixed message per case (for example `kUserGestureRequiredError`,
`kNotInManifestPermissionsError`). Firefox rejects with an `ExtensionError`.
Safari reports a callback/promise error with no named type. This
specification does not define a shared error identity.

Issue: What happens when no window is available to show the prompt in,
separate from the user-activation requirement? Chrome rejects, distinctly
from the no-activation rejection. Firefox has no window requirement and
prompts through whatever surface is available. Safari has no window
concept: the embedding application prompts, and the call resolves `false`
if it never responds.

Chrome and Safari exclude the entire `permissions` namespace from content scripts. Firefox
exposes `request()` alone to content scripts, keeping the rest of the namespace restricted to
background and extension-page contexts.

A [=user agent=] may decline a request without prompting the user, for
example where administrative policy has already denied the permission.

Issue: Does a request declined by policy reject, or resolve `false`?
Chrome and Firefox reject. Safari resolves `false` without prompting.

### `remove()` ### {#permissions-remove}

<dfn method for="permissions">remove(permissions)</dfn> relinquishes some or all of the
extension's optional [=granted permissions=] and [=granted host permissions=].

1. If any named permission or [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is not an [=optional
    permission=] or [=optional host permission=] of the extension, reject with an error.
1. If any named permission or [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is not declared anywhere in the
    extension's manifest, reject with an error.
1. Revoke each named permission in |permissions|["permissions"] that is in the extension's
    [=granted permissions=], and each [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"] that is
    [=subsumed=] by the extension's [=granted host permissions=]. Entries in |permissions| not
    granted are ignored rather than treated as an error. This fires {{permissions/onRemoved}}
    for the revoked set, if any.
1. Resolve with `undefined`.

<div class=issue>
Why does `remove()` resolve with `undefined` here, when every shipping browser resolves `true`?

* All three browsers currently resolve `true` on success.
* [w3c/webextensions#1069](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1069) (open) argues the
    boolean is meaningless, since `remove()` never resolves `false`.
* The sibling case is `alarms.clearAll()`,
    [w3c/webextensions#1055](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1055); the 2026-08-13
    WECG minutes record a Mozilla engineer saying he would be fine dropping its return value too.

This specification follows #1069 rather than current behavior; no browser resolves `undefined`
here today. `request()` and `contains()` are not affected: both resolve a boolean that carries
real information (whether the request was granted, whether the extension has the permission),
so neither changes.
</div>

## Events ## {#permissions-events}

Issue: Should the event-listener pattern (`addListener()`, `removeListener()`, `hasListener()`)
used by every event in this specification be defined once, as a shared `events.Event` type,
rather than duplicated as {{PermissionsEvent}} in each namespace that fires one?

{{PermissionsEvent}} below is a minimal local stand-in pending that decision.

<pre class="idl">
callback PermissionsListener = undefined (optional Permissions changed);

interface PermissionsEvent {
  undefined addListener(PermissionsListener listener);
  undefined removeListener(PermissionsListener listener);
  boolean hasListener(PermissionsListener listener);
};
</pre>

### `onAdded` ### {#permissions-onadded}

The <dfn attribute for="permissions">onAdded</dfn> event fires with a {{Permissions}}
dictionary listing the permissions and origins newly granted to the extension, whenever the
extension's [=granted permissions=] or [=granted host permissions=] increase. This includes grants made through
{{permissions/request}}, and grants made through user agent UI outside of the `permissions` API
(for example, a browser action letting the user grant a withheld host permission).

Chrome explicitly does not fire `onAdded`/`onRemoved` for permission or host-permission grants
driven entirely by administrative or managed policy. Firefox and Safari both disagree with
Chrome here, for different reasons. Firefox's policy-driven permission revocation runs through
the same
internal grant/revoke function the `permissions` API itself calls, with no suppression for the
policy case, so it fires `onRemoved` the same as an API-driven or user-driven change would.
Safari's engine fires `onAdded`/`onRemoved` whenever the embedding application sets the
extension's granted or denied permissions, regardless of why the application did so; since a
managed policy on Safari is applied by the application setting those same properties (see above),
a policy-driven change fires the event there too, unless the application takes some further step
outside the engine to suppress it, which is not something the open-source engine controls one way
or the other.

### `onRemoved` ### {#permissions-onremoved}

The <dfn attribute for="permissions">onRemoved</dfn> event fires with a {{Permissions}}
dictionary listing the permissions and origins no longer granted to the extension, whenever the
extension's [=granted permissions=] or [=granted host permissions=] decrease, whether through {{permissions/remove}} or through user
agent UI.
